\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A time map on a spatial momentum axis}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Take one spatial direction #{x}, one time direction #{t}, a simple complex spin module, trivial links, and the volume grading #{C=\ui\varepsilon_x\varepsilon_t} of \ref{fdip-009X}. On spin use the antiunitary #{c_-} of \ref{fdip-017O}: it negates both directions and squares to #{-\bbI}. On #{F=\mathbb C^2} take #{c_F(z_1,z_2)=(-\cl{z_2},\cl{z_1})}, also of square #{-\bbI}. Use the map of \ref{fdip-0184} with value action #{(\varepsilon_t c_-)\otimes c_F} and reflection of the time coordinate. Its spin factor squares to #{-c_-^2=\bbI}, so the complete antiunitary squares to #{-\bbI}.}

\p{The value action preserves #{\varepsilon_x}, negates #{\varepsilon_t} and conjugates #{\ui} to #{-\ui}, hence fixes #{C}. Time reflection reverses the centered time difference along with its Clifford direction and preserves the Wilson term (\ref{fdip-0184}). Thus this map commutes with #{H=C(D_{W,a,U}-M\bbI)} at every real mass. As in the character calculation of \ref{fdip-0186}, conjugation and site reflection take #{r} to #{-\theta_s r}. For self-opposite time component this equals #{-r}, including the whole spatial axis. At the self-opposite labels in that set the pairs of \ref{fdip-01AO} occur.}
